  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n- Colder temperatures today, with breezy westerly winds. \n\n- Monitoring the potential for weather systems to bring \n  showers/cooler temperatures Wednesday and Friday-Saturday. \n\nMany locations are waking up to colder temperatures this morning,\nwith snow levels falling near 5,000 feet. Radar imagery continues\nto show scattered showers, with light snow accumulations being \nobserved above 7,000 feet along and north of the I-90 corridor. \nLooking ahead to this afternoon, drier air will begin to move into\nthe area under a zonal flow aloft. This will bring an end to \nshower activity, with stratus clouds eroding and high clouds \nmoving overhead. Westerly winds will gradually weaken through the\nmid-afternoon to evening hours across western Montana valley \nareas, while wind gusts of 20-30+ mph continue across the higher \nterrain. \n\nLooking ahead to this upcoming week, the Northern Rockies will\nremain under a zonal westerly flow pattern with high pressure\ncentered across the Desert Southwest and low pressure systems\nfocused north in Canada. A warming trend is expected Tuesday, with\ntemperatures returning near seasonal normals. There's two days to\nmonitor for potential disturbances, bringing an increase in wind\nand shower activity. The first window arrives Tuesday evening into\nWednesday as a shortwave trough moves eastward along the USA/Canada\nborder. Ensemble model guidance has a wide array of solutions with\nhow far southward this trough moves, with currently 80% of\nsolutions suggesting minimal precipitation with scattered shower\nactivity and an increase in westerly winds. The remaining 20% of\nsolutions dig the trough further southward, bringing a modest cold\nfrontal passage and widespread precipitation, focused along and\nnorth of the I-90 corridor. \n\nForecast models continue to show lower confidence in the pattern\nby Friday into Saturday, with regard to a potential low pressure \nsystem. Ensemble cluster analysis shows roughly a 60/40% split,\nwith around 40 percent of model solutions show a much colder and \ndeeper trough, and the remaining 60 percent either showing \ncontinued zonal flow or a weaker trough passage. The majority \nsolution would keep seasonal temperatures, breezy winds, and \nmainly dry conditions, while the deeper trough passage would \nbring a stronger cold front, and widespread shower activity, with\nlowering snow levels down near 5,000-6,000 feet.",
